> I remember a period, maybe when the last generation was graduating, when it became clear that new employees had a different view of their responsibility to their employer. There was a rash of people who had just gotten hired leaving their jobs, or not showing up in the first place. What am I remembering?
**assistant**:
> You’re probably remembering the **post-pandemic labor-market shift of 2021–22**: the **Great Resignation**, alongside widespread reporting on **“employee ghosting.”**
> 
> - Employers reported people accepting offers but never appearing for day one, or quitting very quickly without notice.
> - It was especially associated—sometimes unfairly—with younger workers / Gen Z entering the labor force.
> - Tight labor markets, better outside options, pandemic-era reassessment of work, poor job quality, and low loyalty from employers all contributed.
> - Related labels were **“quiet quitting”** (doing only contractual duties) and, later, **“rage applying.”**
> 
> The underlying cultural claim was that employees—particularly younger ones—no longer owed an employer discretionary loyalty when employers themselves routinely laid people off, offered little security, and expected constant availability. “Ghosting” was real, though media coverage often exaggerated it into a generational morality story.
